<worksheet >
    <title>DC-3 Gradient, Tangent Lines and Linear Approximations</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>To earn an M on this outcome, you will need to correctly complete both problems. 
      To earn a P on this outcome, you will need to correctly complete one part of each problem and show progress on the other parts.</p>
  </introduction>  
  <page>
    <exercise workspace="1.5in">
        <introduction><p>Given the surface <m>g(x,y)=x^2\cos(y)</m>.</p></introduction>
        <task><statement><p>Find the tangent plane to the surface at the point <m>(2,0)</m>.</p></statement></task>
        <task><statement><p>Use the linearization of <m>g</m> at the point <m>(2,0)</m> to approximate <m>g(2.1,-0.2)</m>.</p></statement></task>
    </exercise>
    </page>
    <page>
    <exercise workspace="1.5in">
        <introduction><p>Let <m>f(x,y,z)=x^2y - z^2</m>.</p></introduction>
        <task><statement><p>Find the gradient <m>\nabla f</m> at the point <m>(1,2,2)</m>.</p></statement></task>
        <task><statement><p>Use part (a) to find the directional derivative of <m>f</m> at the point <m>(1,2,2)</m> 
          in the direction of the vector <m>\vec{v}=\langle 2,1,2 \rangle</m>. Use your directional derivative to 
          determine if the function is increasing, decreasing, or stationary at that point in the given direction.</p></statement></task>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>